{"uncertainty": 0.3, "outcome": "Submitted rtl_source skid_buffer.v and testbench skid_buffer_tb.v. They implement the approved two-register skid buffer. No formal_spec is submitted. Lint, simulation, synthesis and review remain with the platform and an independent reviewer.", "artifacts": [{"kind": "design_note", "title": "Implementation decisions for skid_buffer", "summary": "The RTL follows the next-state table in [artifact:prj-46891f716d.microarchitecture.a1]. The registers are m_valid, m_data, skid_valid and skid_data. s_ready = !skid_valid, and m_free = !m_valid || m_ready selects which register loads. Next state is computed in one always @(*) block with a default for every variable. A separate register block holds only reset and the update. Following the microarchitecture's state table, only the two valid bits are reset and the data registers have none. This differs on purpose from the general reset-every-flop guidance, and the outputs agree with [artifact:prj-46891f716d.interface-spec.a1] section 5, which leaves data undefined while m_valid is low. Both upstream documents were independently reviewed: [evidence:prj-46891f716d.interface-spec.e1] [evidence:prj-46891f716d.microarchitecture.e1]. Traces to [requirement:req-49d207f970]."}, {"kind": "deviation_record", "title": "Formal proof setup not submitted with this task", "summary": "Section 5 of [artifact:prj-46891f716d.microarchitecture.a1] plans two job files, skid_buffer.sby at WIDTH 8 and skid_buffer_w1.sby at WIDTH 1. The platform accepts exactly one formal_spec per task, so both cannot be submitted here. This task's expected outputs are rtl_source and testbench only. I therefore did not submit an untested proof setup. WIDTH 1 is covered in simulation by a second lane instead. The formal requirement of [artifact:prj-46891f716d.interface-spec.a1] section 6 stays open and needs a follow-up decision."}], "files": [{"path": "skid_buffer.v", "kind": "rtl_source", "title": "skid_buffer RTL (register slice, parameter WIDTH)", "summary": "Implements the ports and parameter of [artifact:prj-46891f716d.interface-spec.a1] section 2. It uses the two-register structure and the next-state table of [artifact:prj-46891f716d.microarchitecture.a1] section 3. s_ready is the inverse of the skid_valid flop, and m_valid and m_data are flops, so no path runs combinationally from an input to an output. Reset is synchronous and active low and clears only the valid bits. Plain Verilog-2001, with no latches by construction (every combinational variable has a default). Serves [requirement:req-49d207f970].", "entry": "skid_buffer"}, {"path": "skid_buffer_tb.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking testbench for skid_buffer, lanes at WIDTH 8 and 1", "summary": "Instantiates the slice at WIDTH 8 and at WIDTH 1. Both lanes share the same handshakes, and the WIDTH 1 lane takes bit 0 of the data. A clock driven from a task gives one tick per cycle. In every cycle a queue model of up to two beats checks m_valid, s_ready, m_data (the oldest beat), the hold rule and that the two lanes agree. Before each edge the testbench flips s_valid and m_ready for one time unit and checks that s_ready does not move. The checks follow the verification plan of [artifact:prj-46891f716d.microarchitecture.a1] section 5. Directed sections cover the list in [artifact:prj-46891f716d.interface-spec.a1] section 6:\n- reset values\n- one-cycle latency\n- 64-beat full throughput\n- a stall with one beat held and with two\n- ready rising after a stall\n- streams with one-cycle and multi-cycle stalls\n- reset with two beats held and with one\n- 4000 cycles of random traffic with a reset during traffic, ending in a drain and a beat-accounting check\n\nFailures are reported with $error. Passes are counted, and the NIRMAAN-SIM verdict is printed once before $finish.", "entry": "skid_buffer_tb"}], "tool_runs": [], "claims": ["I have not run the testbench or the RTL through any tool. Lint, simulation and synthesis results come only from the platform's runs.", "I expect the RTL to be Verilator -Wall clean at WIDTH 8 and 1 and to synthesize in Yosys with no latches. This expectation is unverified.", "I expect the testbench to build under both Icarus Verilog and Verilator, for example with --binary and its timing support. Its widths are sized to avoid fatal WIDTH warnings. This is unverified.", "Under Verilator's default error limit, the first $error may stop the run before the verdict. A failing check then still produces a failed run."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Two open points for review.\n\n1. Formal setup: the microarchitecture plans two .sby files, but this task's platform accepts one formal_spec. I left formal to a follow-up task. That task should submit skid_buffer.sby at WIDTH 8 in prove mode, with a depth long enough for the covers. WIDTH 1 stays covered by the simulation lane. The tech lead (design.rtl.design.tech_lead) should confirm this split.\n\n2. Data registers are not reset, as the approved state table specifies. A reviewer applying the reset-every-flop rule should treat this as a recorded decision, not a gap.\n\nThe testbench drives the clock from a task, so every check is placed at a deterministic point in the cycle. The model follows the DUT's own s_ready to decide acceptance, and it flags any mismatch separately."}

=== FILE: skid_buffer.v ===
// skid_buffer: valid/ready register slice with one skid register.
// Implements interface_spec.md and microarchitecture.md (skid_buffer).
// Every output is a flop or the inverse of one; inputs reach only next state.
`default_nettype none

module skid_buffer #(
    parameter integer WIDTH = 8
) (
    input  wire             clk,
    input  wire             rst_n,
    input  wire             s_valid,
    output wire             s_ready,
    input  wire [WIDTH-1:0] s_data,
    output reg              m_valid,
    input  wire             m_ready,
    output reg  [WIDTH-1:0] m_data
);

    // Skid register: holds the newer of two beats.
    reg             skid_valid;
    reg [WIDTH-1:0] skid_data;

    // Next-state signals.
    reg             m_valid_d;
    reg [WIDTH-1:0] m_data_d;
    reg             m_load;
    reg             skid_valid_d;
    reg             skid_load;

    wire m_free;
    wire s_fire;

    assign s_ready = ~skid_valid;
    assign m_free  = ~m_valid | m_ready;
    assign s_fire  = s_valid & s_ready;

    always @(*) begin
        m_valid_d    = m_valid;
        m_data_d     = s_data;
        m_load       = 1'b0;
        skid_valid_d = skid_valid;
        skid_load    = 1'b0;
        if (m_free) begin
            if (skid_valid) begin
                // The skid beat moves to the output register.
                m_valid_d    = 1'b1;
                m_data_d     = skid_data;
                m_load       = 1'b1;
                skid_valid_d = 1'b0;
            end else begin
                // The output register loads the input beat, if any.
                m_valid_d    = s_valid;
                m_data_d     = s_data;
                m_load       = 1'b1;
            end
        end else if (s_fire) begin
            // Output stalled and a beat accepted: it goes to the skid register.
            skid_valid_d = 1'b1;
            skid_load    = 1'b1;
        end
    end

    // Control registers: synchronous active-low reset.
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (!rst_n) begin
            m_valid    <= 1'b0;
            skid_valid <= 1'b0;
        end else begin
            m_valid    <= m_valid_d;
            skid_valid <= skid_valid_d;
        end
    end

    // Data registers: not reset (microarchitecture section 2); load enables only.
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (m_load) begin
            m_data <= m_data_d;
        end
        if (skid_load) begin
            skid_data <= s_data;
        end
    end

endmodule

`default_nettype wire
=== END FILE ===
